[{"data":1,"prerenderedAt":274},["ShallowReactive",2],{"news:\u002Fnews\u002F2012-01-06-a-lunar-update-trick":3,"news-timeline":55},{"id":4,"title":5,"author":6,"body":7,"date":45,"description":46,"extension":47,"meta":48,"navigation":49,"path":50,"readingTime":51,"seo":52,"stem":53,"__hash__":54},"news\u002Fnews\u002F2012-01-06-a-lunar-update-trick.md","A `lunar update` trick","dveatch",{"type":8,"value":9,"toc":41},"minimark",[10,14,22,29,32,38],[11,12,13],"p",{},"There are many things I love about Lunar and here is a little trick that might save you some time.",[11,15,16,17,21],{},"When ",[18,19,20],"code",{},"lunar update"," is run it will of course sort through the modules installed on your system and present you with an editable list. As a Lunar developer, often times I know a module in that list will fail its build because an optional dependency has changed. Sometimes I forget this until staring at the list and think \"Oh poo, I know that one will fail\".",[11,23,24,25,28],{},"My first option might be to let the update run and tidy things up later. My second option might be; do a Ctrl C, then ",[18,26,27],{},"lin -rc $MODULE"," and rerun the update.  Yes, we are aware Lunar can do a better job of figuring out when a modules optional dependencies have been changed (it is on our ToDo list).",[11,30,31],{},"To say the least, interrupting an update is a bit annoying. So if you want to reconfigure the optional depends of a module while in edit mode, just add \"-rc\" to the beginning of that modules line. Using phonon as an example, just make it look like this;",[11,33,34],{},[35,36,37],"strong",{},"-rc phonon",[11,39,40],{},"And that's it. Once the list is saved, Lunar will ask you again which optional depends it should use.",{"title":42,"searchDepth":43,"depth":43,"links":44},"",3,[],"2012-01-06","Add -rc to a line in the lunar update list to reconfigure that module's optional dependencies.","md",{},true,"\u002Fnews\u002F2012-01-06-a-lunar-update-trick",2,{"title